IO-07: do not treat a ';' inside a quoted token as a comment

getTokens() cut the line at the first ';' before it looked for quoted
tokens, so a quoted file name containing ';' (TS1 FILE "flow;v2.dat") was
cut short and the file could not be opened (ERROR 361). The comment is now
found by scanning the line and skipping over a quoted token, i.e. a '"' at
the start of a token with a closing '"' later on the line, which is the same
rule the tokenizer uses. A line without such a quoted token is cut at its
first ';' exactly as before.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply IO-07_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/input.c b/src/legacy/engine/input.c
index 5a58319..746ba42 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/input.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/input.c
@@ -884,9 +884,18 @@ int getTokens(char *s)
     for (n = 0; n < MAXTOKS; n++) Tok[n] = NULL;
     n = 0;
 
-    // --- truncate s at start of comment 
-    c = strchr(s,';');
-    if (c) *c = '\0';
+    // --- truncate s at start of comment (a ';' inside a quoted token,
+    //     e.g. a file name, is part of the token)
+    for (c = s; *c; c++)
+    {
+        if ( *c == '"' && (c == s || strchr(SEPSTR, c[-1])) &&
+             strchr(c+1, '"') ) c = strchr(c+1, '"');
+        else if ( *c == ';' )
+        {
+            *c = '\0';
+            break;
+        }
+    }
     len = (int)strlen(s);
 
     // --- scan s for tokens until nothing left
